        "text": "D.1 BOUNDS ON THE HESSIAN\nIn this section, we provide bounds on the Hessian of the perturbative loss L1\u03c4 . We first express the loss as a function of the covariance matrix, in which case the Hessian is known (Kroshnin et al., 2021). Then, a simple chain rule for the differential allows to express the Hessian in the case the loss is a function of the end-to-end matrix W . Lemma D.1 (Second-order differential of L\u03031\u03c4 , Kroshnin et al. 2021, Lemma A.2). Let W \u2208 Rn\u00d7m and let \u03c4 > 0. Define \u03a3\u03c4 = WW\u22a4 + \u03c4In to be the regularized covariance matrix. Given that \u03a3\u03c4 \u227b 0, the loss 9 is twice continuously differentiable for any W . Let \u0393Q\u0393\u22a4 = \u03a31/20 \u03a3\u03c4\u03a3 1/2 0 be a spectral decomposition of \u03a31/20 \u03a3\u03c4\u03a3 1/2 0 , with Q = diag (q1, . . . , qn). For Y \u2208 Rn\u00d7n, define\n\u2206(Y ) \u2208 Rn\u00d7n to be the matrix with element \u2206(Y )ij = ( (\u0393\u22a4Y \u0393)ij\u221a qi+ \u221a qj ) . Let H\u0303 be the linear operator defined as H\u0303(Y ) = \u03a3\n1/2 0 \u0393Q \u22121/2\u2206(Y )Q\u22121/2\u0393\u22a4\u03a3 1/2 0 . (39)\nThen, the second order differential of L\u03031\u03c4 is given by\n\u2200(X,Y ) \u2208 (Rn\u00d7n)2, d2L\u03031\u03c4 (\u03a3\u03c4 )[X,Y ] = \u27e8X, H\u0303(Y )\u27e9. (40)\nProof. We begin by stating the first-order differential for the loss L\u03031 evaluated on the PD matrix \u03a3\u03c4 . This is given in lemma C.10\ndL\u03031(\u03a3\u03c4 )[X] = tr(X \u2212 \u03a31/20 (\u03a3 1/2 0 \u03a3\u03c4\u03a3 1/2 0 )\n\u22121/2 \u03a3\n1/2 0 X)\n= \u27e8I \u2212 \u03a31/20 (\u03a3 1/2 0 \u03a3\u03c4\u03a3 1/2 0 )\n\u22121/2 \u03a3\n1/2 0 , X\u27e9.\nLet GL(n) = { A \u2208 Rn\u00d7n | detA \u0338= 0 } , and let f : GL(n) \u220b F 7\u2192 F\u22121; then f is differentiable with differential df(F )[X] = \u2212F\u22121XF\u22121 (Magnus & Neudecker, 2019, Theorem 8.3). Let g : Sn++ \u220b A 7\u2192 A1/2 be the matrix square 